## How your system works
3-5 sentences: the whole system in plain words — the software your team and clients touch, the AI working inside it, and where your information lives. No layer-cake jargon; an owner reads this and can repeat it to a colleague.

## The parts, one by one
One ### subsection PER MODULE, in the decomposition's order. Each subsection restates that module's "spec" and "tech" facts in the owner's language — do not invent beyond them; omit any line whose underlying data is missing:
- **What this part does:** 1-2 plain sentences (from purpose/features)
- **What it keeps track of:** each record from tech.data_model as a plain phrase with what's inside, e.g. "every pre-approval request — which patient, which insurer, which treatments, where it stands". Never show snake_case or field-list notation here.
- **The screens you'll use:** from the spec ("screens")
- **What the AI does on its own:** the agent's purpose and its tools translated into plain verbs, e.g. "checks where a request stands, drafts the message to the patient, flags anything unusual" — or the line "No AI in this part — deliberately." when tech.ai_agent is null
- **What it knows:** the agent's brain in plain words — the knowledge of YOUR business it is grounded in
- **Where it stops and hands to you:** decides_alone vs escalation, as one honest sentence naming who picks it up
- **What it will never do:** the guardrails, plainly
- **How you'll know it's working:** evaluation + the module's KPIs as things the owner can watch
- **How this part gets built, in order:** tech.build_sequence translated into numbered plain steps ("first the records it keeps, then the workflow, then the AI wired in, then the screens" — but specific to this module)
- **It's finished when:** tech.done_when verbatim in spirit — checks the owner can personally verify
- **For your build team:** ONE compact line with the technical identifiers a developer needs: record names, tool names, connection points (from tech.data_model/tools/apis/integration_details). This is the only place technical notation is allowed.

## Applying it, step by step
3-4 phases, sequenced by dependency. Use EXACTLY this bullet format per phase (top-level bullet, two indented sub-bullets):
*   **Phase 1: <name>**
    *   **Delivers:** <which parts from above, by name>
    *   **You can already:** <what the owner can personally do and see working at the end of this phase>
No durations, no dates, no prices.

## If you build it with your own team
This plan is complete enough to execute without us — this section tells the owner how to USE this document with any competent team. Cover, as short labeled bullets:
- **How to brief them:** hand over this document; the "For your build team" lines and the build orders are their starting point
- **Team shape:** the smallest team that can build this (roles, not names), adjusted to THIS system's real complexity
- **Build order:** the parts in dependency order, one line of reasoning each
- **Buy, don't build:** the pieces to take off the shelf (messaging, calendars, hosting, sign-in) as categories with what to look for — a vendor example or two is fine, never a pitch
- **How to check their work:** use each part's "It's finished when" checks — you don't need to read code to verify them
- **Trusting the AI before it goes live:** human review of everything the AI produces first; autonomy only after the evaluation checks pass
- **The first thing to ship:** the smallest end-to-end slice that touches real users, and why starting there de-risks everything else

## Keeping your information safe
4-5 bullets, system-wide, in plain words: where your data lives, backups, client privacy, who can see what, staying available — specific to this business, no boilerplate.
When any module touches payments, settlements, payouts or other sensitive financial records, these are the floor and must be covered: sign-in stronger than a phone number or order code before financial records are shown; each person and client sees only their own records (a business client never sees another client's money; an end customer never sees a merchant's settlement details); every access and every adjustment to a financial record is logged; financial adjustments need a named human's approval, and the person who adjusts is not the person who approves.

